045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402336} {:answer/id #uuid "c6754a7b-081e-5d31-992c-2069f9dc057c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402336} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b9f9176-6990-5c20-b84c-e134dd361960" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9937b4b8-0233-5d12-b8c0-111d5bfd92fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343445" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi )=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1+\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ -4 & =-1+\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bc78a68d-4643-5a86-980c-c8ebf837e55e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06015b37-c1da-5c8e-878c-60947fbc840c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315148} {:answer/id #uuid "24ea2114-3bd1-52c4-bf57-325363fd5bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315325} {:answer/id #uuid "a81de909-9548-5ec4-8d3c-1983bf705587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c781efb-4362-5ebb-94b7-8bf35aea1ea4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ed29729-d42b-5f9d-81b4-fac91b47193e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315651} {:answer/id #uuid "70cfcbc1-b8b9-5044-9c8f-66a69b6cebb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315754}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315754} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a481c8-ee27-5287-8fe1-cf0b98ba368c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316779} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f9e4870-e628-563e-a443-273a044c29a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315483} {:answer/id #uuid "49981036-e986-5f68-b872-080b6058e36b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315541} {:answer/id #uuid "66f98de7-7d60-5034-8d02-7bcf4b8711fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315693} {:answer/id #uuid "94932d9a-77a0-52a3-8808-65e5fb4787b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315483} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdd1db35-8a48-5144-bd44-16bb2accace4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4117204a-c411-5a81-a516-319a51947f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315496} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8df8b2-20ed-52bb-9e87-9be21ab82a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315571} {:answer/id #uuid "5057327a-0af6-5793-85ba-f785f549d306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315576} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc542ce-59d5-5839-85d3-c4e505bc822a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315571} :answer-field/id #uuid "c245d06f-5900-5a46-89d5-dbf08d5da9c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e47b2683-e120-52c6-81fc-c52d398d6446" :question/math-academy-id "q-343421" :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (4{n}^{2}-1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{0}{2})|=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\pi } & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dda307b5-8003-57b0-9aa5-1bc31da204b7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506649}}] :activity/title "Convergence of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505905}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511625} :activity/id #uuid "7561226a-c4c4-531a-b031-f64d92fb4a30" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050989 :topic/id #uuid "7742668d-ad15-5c25-bc82-4ea5b4fd800d" :topic/math-academy-id 3198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f6f980b-262a-50f0-ae3d-21301e83956a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402304} {:answer/id #uuid "c9091f78-a863-5b79-82af-d78f1b7a6e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402305} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2405a2-3755-5df5-98e5-3e6b5d35f747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402306} {:answer/id #uuid "81d46c72-93bb-5c34-b05d-8f8e24f569a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402307} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea007838-d48e-5282-9c26-7807b59be905" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc209ef-e0e4-5126-a4d3-28477eac6672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402309} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a6dfee-6b51-51e4-878f-80f01834b392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402310} {:answer/id #uuid "89861f72-3267-5a53-91e4-553173ff8b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402311} {:answer/id #uuid "37400167-26ee-5154-ac19-3de49ebc5692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402311} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2df7cda-b6f1-52c3-94de-e01cfc959b14" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaad6fdf-5307-581d-a51e-86eb719eaa00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402314} {:answer/id #uuid "1129b323-9145-5bfc-8573-2c423509d406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402314} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabd5536-e333-5913-8a91-953c55edab0a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8f13b00-9c43-5ce3-9ea2-b89ef353d34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402317} {:answer/id #uuid "5794e73f-ea5d-524c-bb11-82c1cf90b93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402318} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9629e5-251b-531d-8097-ef9b42974051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402319}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402319} :answer-field/id #uuid "240e5c16-d6bd-5404-b856-e008054e31c9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ea02f42-e1ec-509f-a6b0-19fd2f4064ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-343438" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi )=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4}) \\\\ -1 & =1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "05862192-baca-5966-ba59-871832f53fa7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864ead19-4057-5f6a-8c06-48fb2c7562de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511059} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd05ee6-ac91-5d21-8ecf-c0b6a272af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511059} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de15137-bf56-596a-a38a-ac01796200ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e66d128-cec6-555a-8143-73ec6e9676c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510878} {:answer/id #uuid "8e23dd8e-fbc6-5790-9b1d-fe0b23c91599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511075} {:answer/id #uuid "df2fadf9-ffc9-50bc-80ad-3b43d5a65713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511244} {:answer/id #uuid "e374467d-7122-514c-8ff6-30b09a2ff344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511244} :answer-field/id #uuid "253cae4c-287a-57dd-940c-b84a887327b5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be46685-8a2f-512e-8ecc-5f60c5fb734b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510975} {:answer/id #uuid "a6eff1ea-c328-5cf5-ba20-f5e842a11bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511135} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d2133a-3545-57a6-b6ce-a65c4be200ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511135} :answer-field/id #uuid "8864a19e-a3dc-5d3a-b104-94d3cbc88d3b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aabc6bb-ca4a-5cb9-9ccd-4e3aecda5a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510916} {:answer/id #uuid "4a723052-6e2a-5c5d-a43a-4a384522e9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510946} {:answer/id #uuid "baf10e71-ca8e-5c28-b131-42d39b4e9fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511165} {:answer/id #uuid "dde8b71b-c391-5449-aef8-6a80127dccaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510916} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a017d0-45f8-582e-844c-12a17fcdf030" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ad7e028-7ba2-59c0-89a8-df188134628e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343448" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "25c32ee0-cde1-52dc-a55d-04342210f54d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "735db7a5-7bcb-5fa9-a24e-2f32aa107ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511023} :answer-field/id #uuid "790c545a-a448-5b09-a252-ea7895e22766" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ddf2fa7b-cdfe-5039-9760-76db03b3d4cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-342505" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{1+0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5d01adcd-f270-5af1-9222-71935e9dab45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb5a35bc-6e98-5466-b248-220b58805210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375036} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e9342f-7d4b-572d-ae06-c85b2c42d4ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8508b9f1-644b-5b60-9272-323a48701a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343367" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{0+2}{2}=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ab5a7fdd-1411-570c-8898-03bed4d4b76d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2acba6b-85a0-5271-998e-5cb3d652c89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375057} :answer-field/id #uuid "05225483-6672-597f-a26b-7bc809c08b7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a441f1e0-bba8-5858-84bf-38b79256c3c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343451" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2}=\\frac{2+(-4)}{2}=-1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af8c258e-ccf8-5173-8bbf-21e285594589"}] :activity/title "Convergence of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510762}]}
